#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119708 :question/id #uuid "1b4b69f7-fc4f-57c3-bcaf-9df98e4fc5e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8947" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx = u, \\quad \ny = v, \\quad \nz = 1 - u - v\n$$\n\nfor $-1 \\leq u \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u, \\: v, \\: 1 - u - v \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD = \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9361ad5a-b15c-507b-85d4-fe7ccb4d9fd8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207 :topic/math-academy-id 1992 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Rectangular Domains"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/math-academy-id 2000 :topic/title "Properties of Double Integrals"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bfe5eea-2ed5-57d1-920c-5bf7749bd52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238163} {:answer/id #uuid "28eb82e3-6a9c-5de4-9c73-f476cef15973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238416} {:answer/id #uuid "44d82293-ae28-5a3b-91f7-6f335e4e5f9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238652} {:answer/id #uuid "5574b951-f5f1-5ffb-80db-fad8029179eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238793} {:answer/id #uuid "e1123ef7-3b43-5120-a22c-5cde4bfdb285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bfe5eea-2ed5-57d1-920c-5bf7749bd52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238163} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc9f1b5d-bcf1-568c-bbaf-58ab6b82ef80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240772}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04aeca54-3b07-58c4-888a-6b39f758095a" :question/math-academy-id "q-105920" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=2u-v,\\,y=3u+2,\\,z=u+2v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $-1\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u-v,\\,3u+2,\\,u+2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u+2),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,3,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u+2),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,-5,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨6,\\,-5,\\,3⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+25+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{70}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}{∬}_{D}\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "411bf46a-dda1-51f0-bd59-b820659afb72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{57}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238619} {:answer/id #uuid "6900a7b8-7d5a-562e-9c9d-ad769c178da7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238966} {:answer/id #uuid "83aef3ae-1313-5321-a641-f9fa2ba69ce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239202} {:answer/id #uuid "87703ec0-9ddc-5d7d-a758-6baa7dc46d31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239232} {:answer/id #uuid "d7def3e5-8bf5-5eb5-99c2-b1acdcaf5acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83aef3ae-1313-5321-a641-f9fa2ba69ce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239202} :answer-field/id #uuid "accfc2a2-378a-5435-9ff9-d8a31a34f2e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240634}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fae50a6d-3a64-5292-bf93-f5341ecb7d4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105922" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨-u+v,\\,u-2v,\\,3v⟩$ that is subject to the constraint ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,3,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨3,\\,3,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 4$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 4},\n$$\n\nof radius $2.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{19}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ee8c68-1864-57de-a946-4d5491f72162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244826} {:answer/id #uuid "3c6058cd-ef3c-5b04-9408-d57007317bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244843} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2a11de-f795-5b2e-9701-89f49ea4e8e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245018} {:answer/id #uuid "55f22ff4-a11d-5608-9228-8c9c4e21d127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245070} {:answer/id #uuid "af8101ed-0f01-5edf-b92b-9155125b12fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f2a11de-f795-5b2e-9701-89f49ea4e8e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245018} :answer-field/id #uuid "97488496-77bc-5262-b42c-1231cd7a5226" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246811}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84f10468-a9ec-54bf-a4d6-59a03d61dbaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-109710" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=2-3u,\\,z=1+u-v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $-1\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,2-3u,\\,1+u-v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2-3u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-3,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2-3u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨3,\\,2,\\,3⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{22}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a511b6f-0600-5198-a79a-e5527e8f7af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244549} {:answer/id #uuid "781bc53d-6da8-596b-92ef-3b236df69123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245380} {:answer/id #uuid "97a2dde0-64ef-5072-b3c5-71c64fc80cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245651} {:answer/id #uuid "cfbef9b2-a6dc-5bfa-ae03-27e59b80192b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246168} {:answer/id #uuid "f925caff-0904-5fa2-afc1-5cc9e4eb7a19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a511b6f-0600-5198-a79a-e5527e8f7af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244549} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2a4171-2411-5190-8229-ee37e0db2e69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246568}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d492e8b2-2ae8-555e-8788-3c26b7ebc400" :question/math-academy-id "q-109712" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y={v}^{2},\\,z=\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le \\sqrt{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{v}^{2},\\,\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,2{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 2{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2v & 2{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2{v}^{2},\\,2v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\,-2{v}^{2},\\,2v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2{v}^{2})^{2}+(2v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{4}+4{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}({v}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{{v}^{2}}=v$ since $v\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le \\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nw={v}^{2}+1\\,⇒\\,\\text{d}w=2v\\,\\text{d}v,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{w}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{w}^{3}}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81df853c-c7b7-503a-81f4-854a8d9dd531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245462} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a7eecb-208c-5bb4-b7a1-8c9244d1069f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245822} {:answer/id #uuid "e86c003f-fdb5-59bd-8dc8-507c0b72a262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246370} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b0d156-8308-5819-9741-f9503e77ca41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246503} {:answer/id #uuid "fdfd09ae-23c2-518b-a998-39c77888edd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8a7eecb-208c-5bb4-b7a1-8c9244d1069f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245822} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd5766b3-0604-500f-bffc-f2b07efa78de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246941}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c206356-09e7-589f-8fd6-4bbad41aebab" :question/math-academy-id "q-109761" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=uv,\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $1\\le v\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,uv,\\,\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,v,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,u,\\,v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & v & 0 \\\\ 0 & u & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}v & 0 \\\\ u & v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{v}^{2},\\,-2uv,\\,2{u}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨{v}^{2},\\,-2uv,\\,2{u}^{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({v}^{2})^{2}+(-2uv)^{2}+(2{u}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{u}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({v}^{2}+2{u}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & ={v}^{2}+2{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({v}^{2}+2{u}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({v}^{2}+2{u}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[u{v}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({v}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}+\\frac{2}{3}v]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{2}^{3}+2(2)-({1}^{3}+2(1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(9) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2e4bdd-442c-5db5-9f63-c23adc2689fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244690} {:answer/id #uuid "49dc1ed9-4cad-5a2c-8301-d22f556db3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244971} {:answer/id #uuid "6621db82-2238-537b-9c86-6e6889b3eb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245213} {:answer/id #uuid "7060d728-881c-574f-9bb2-5498f2a3deb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245320} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4ca8a3-27e9-57c9-af53-8832d20f6d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7060d728-881c-574f-9bb2-5498f2a3deb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245320} :answer-field/id #uuid "15dadaf4-eeb3-594f-bfbb-198fd6c49858" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246585}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "41677fe6-6daf-5332-9eef-d0840faca5fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-109779" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u-v⟩$ that is subject to the constraint ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 9.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 9$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 9},\n$$\n\nof radius $3.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}) \\\\  & =9\\pi \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025f0489-87f5-5a9f-add3-b7a1173218b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244328} {:answer/id #uuid "091b32cf-3a1d-5b7c-bdc7-3d5f6a69cd98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244389} {:answer/id #uuid "5b48dc53-794d-5288-8722-cc17c2171077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245113} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c5345d-feb7-5bcf-a354-6148292fa771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245829} {:answer/id #uuid "f3113b15-3a1a-584d-913c-08a25612137a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3113b15-3a1a-584d-913c-08a25612137a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246461} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e01c6e8-5c98-5791-a262-2b4a7162b8c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246772}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "266cc575-6c30-52b6-9198-190d4fbe9593" :question/math-academy-id "q-110252" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,1-2u+5v⟩$ that is subject to the constraint ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-2u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-2u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-5,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\,-5,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{(-5)}^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 2$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 2}\n$$\n\nof radius $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{30}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ab3c21a-7901-5e8a-b919-8ac158d0b3ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244832} {:answer/id #uuid "4a1afc8b-e18c-59b0-8629-409f8bad9d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244973} {:answer/id #uuid "7e300a0f-c019-5688-aa90-d9d3c280a735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245430} {:answer/id #uuid "94156068-3295-59c4-b393-88ea63372de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245608} {:answer/id #uuid "c23438eb-159b-5522-8561-54d501b4334c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a1afc8b-e18c-59b0-8629-409f8bad9d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244973} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a678cbb-8af1-5c41-a2b0-0bb51a36b874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246669}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3426a71b-5b0e-56f2-83bf-dae50aa51435" :question/math-academy-id "q-110551" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\,u+2v,\\,2u-v⟩$ that is subject to the constraint ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,\\,-1,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-5,\\,-1,\\,3⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+1+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 1$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 1},\n$$\n\nof radius $1.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{35}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119889 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119709 :question/id #uuid "4b202b4e-d8dc-524c-8cb4-ab0c00eb78cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8958" :question/problem "![](images/95/95e3f2da04e23c9b42ea04cba3ee1d7cc069fef992592f09a70cef367b670f0d.png)\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: v, \\: u^3-v \\big\\rangle$ for $0 \\leq u \\leq 3$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,3{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{u}^{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-3{u}^{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3{u}^{2})^{2}+(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{u}^{4}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{9{u}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2928b0de-fadb-5ffa-b30f-72c0051f0ac6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "054fe519-ab8b-5f85-ab9a-d3d159cab8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}u\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡v+8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238123} {:answer/id #uuid "79000f71-931f-5f6e-9579-ea3d7e92e42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239098} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4d4cc8-79ec-5532-8e6c-1a5240849a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v+{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239569} {:answer/id #uuid "ee50303c-a270-5c12-a05d-7f037348e740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240154} {:answer/id #uuid "f622f3d7-b438-53d7-9272-6937b0c0643b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v+4{u}^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "054fe519-ab8b-5f85-ab9a-d3d159cab8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}u\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡v+8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238123} :answer-field/id #uuid "f15110c7-0c2f-5ea3-8cdf-522b5ef09c66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240754}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4d337cb-e76c-55f1-8712-82ae25a0b484" :question/math-academy-id "q-105613" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi }\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le \\pi .$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & 1 \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & 1 \\\\ -3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 1 \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-6u{sin}^{2}⁡v-6u{cos}^{2}⁡v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-6u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-6u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)^{2}+(2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)^{2}+(-6u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v+4{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(5{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v+4{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v)+4{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v+4{u}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v)+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v+4{u}^{2}+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v+40{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡v+8)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡v+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}u\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡v+8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{{u}^{2}}=u$ since $u\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{5}u\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡v+8}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "319007b2-b622-521a-9fbd-6b55411ba8fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244758} {:answer/id #uuid "77d27a23-474c-5fd8-b89a-b5cf94e06791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u\\sqrt{1+4v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245376} {:answer/id #uuid "93b89d68-2ae3-5725-a00d-dea53dab7aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245602} {:answer/id #uuid "a4251703-6830-575d-b816-b1664fbed324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245780} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcccb75-fd9a-544a-9328-801afbd6a123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "319007b2-b622-521a-9fbd-6b55411ba8fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244758} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bbe3440-ff66-5a42-bcf4-05a3f9473d00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246790}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a126827b-bc56-516e-a030-80e53d51bfcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-109941" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,u-{v}^{2},\\,{v}^{2}⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2v,\\,2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2v & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2v & 2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2v,\\,-4uv,\\,-4uv⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2v,\\,-4uv,\\,-4uv⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2v)^{2}+(-4uv)^{2}+(-4uv)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+32{u}^{2}{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}(1+8{u}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{{v}^{2}}=v$ since $v\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e41b296-1fe0-5842-854b-f480f12525fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u}\\sqrt{u+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245145} {:answer/id #uuid "87cd26ad-a962-56ea-bdee-5e602d62af6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2u}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245510} {:answer/id #uuid "97c12a1b-9a2b-5781-8bcb-d79f575b9c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245655} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a80cc0-881d-5ed4-b72e-87eb79fb4fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245752} {:answer/id #uuid "a67a985a-4d91-553c-91f4-71e58ab8a6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97c12a1b-9a2b-5781-8bcb-d79f575b9c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245655} :answer-field/id #uuid "31c52917-76e8-5845-8126-fd7d10803407" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246628}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3edff3d6-2a79-51e6-bf36-1af5081d0991" :question/math-academy-id "q-109948" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi }\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 4$ and $0\\le v\\le \\pi .$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 0 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 0 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{2u}({sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v),\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{2u},\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-{e}^{2u},\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-{e}^{2u})^{2}+({e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)^{2}+({e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{4u}+{e}^{2u}{sin}^{2}⁡v+{e}^{2u}{cos}^{2}⁡v} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{4u}+{e}^{2u}({sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{4u}+{e}^{2u}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}({e}^{2u}+1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{u}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247071 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aba1084-dd62-5f6d-8414-67325a66d2b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244408} {:answer/id #uuid "3262fefb-ad44-55d0-9d7c-75f64ce5ef28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+2{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244764} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7dc017-c361-5e32-9ac8-b550df86db3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244996} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b7b5f0-4589-56a5-a613-5fdf9f4f5425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245804} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a8dda4-db10-5e30-883a-9f6d8c1666a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2a8dda4-db10-5e30-883a-9f6d8c1666a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246050} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e682a7f-849f-592f-9a4e-0dc921cdf99a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246823}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae0a4a11-25db-5593-ba23-9a81fd0d6c49" :question/math-academy-id "q-110019" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi }\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le \\pi .$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 0 & 2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 0 & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 0 & 2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u{sin}^{2}⁡v-2u{cos}^{2}⁡v,\\,2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,4{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\,2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,4{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-2u,\\,2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,4{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2u)^{2}+(2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)^{2}+(4{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+4{u}^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡v+16{u}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡v} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+4{u}^{4}({sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v)+12{u}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡v} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+4{u}^{4}+12{u}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡v} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}(1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v} \\\\  & =2u\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{{u}^{2}}=u$ since $u\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2u\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e335f1ce-f85b-55fb-9898-0984e81e5a0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397959} {:answer/id #uuid "058877bc-b659-5e3e-87cf-faeec5fcf705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397960} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8caafa-3ec3-522c-ad87-8c146d142f74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397961} {:answer/id #uuid "354ff6f5-50a5-5ef5-9619-794c2e23d89b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397962} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e0a436-d109-5391-b4da-ce44a96c863e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8e0a436-d109-5391-b4da-ce44a96c863e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397963} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dc7b100-8716-5ef0-ac33-e0d012d77654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397958}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8259cc12-8425-5d9e-923a-3106edaae5d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109943" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,3-v,\\,1+4u+5v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+4u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+4u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨4,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5545b0b7-751d-5798-9bd4-dfa8faf23f03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397966} {:answer/id #uuid "a470c598-2c0a-5ceb-ac0e-1fb73e235141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u\\sqrt{9{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397967} {:answer/id #uuid "dade9321-241a-5817-902c-2195520cffda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397968} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7e02df-d389-5236-a15d-ad10a54c5136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{18{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397969} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a959ef-e722-5be5-9bcf-f1870eca1b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e7e02df-d389-5236-a15d-ad10a54c5136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{18{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397969} :answer-field/id #uuid "96083256-eb07-5ebd-a86e-e5865f463363" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397965}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c235bda4-b647-59f5-b137-7fc5bc86576a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109947" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,3{u}^{2},\\,u-v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,6u,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 6u & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}6u & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 6u \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6u,\\,2,\\,-6u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-6u,\\,2,\\,-6u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6u)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(-6u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{u}^{2}+4+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72{u}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(18{u}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{18{u}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\sqrt{18{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a16278f-a652-5ce3-b9f8-9f80d8b88232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397973} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1a6ff8-553d-5774-99ac-38531f54fba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397974} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d50f4f-f2ad-5974-83b1-c8764b04094b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397975} {:answer/id #uuid "c4340729-f530-5302-81b0-b5b30177bfba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397976} {:answer/id #uuid "21efbbd9-845c-5789-8305-5927df10c402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a1a6ff8-553d-5774-99ac-38531f54fba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397974} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b095c98-f0c4-52f0-b54c-c5176a3e6c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397972}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0aca92da-c648-5c8b-ae46-3b81f8b99c27" :question/math-academy-id "q-109951" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 4}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,uv⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 4.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & v \\\\ 2v & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-2{u}^{2},\\,4uv⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-2{u}^{2},\\,4uv⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2{v}^{2})^{2}+(-2{u}^{2})^{2}+(4uv)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}+16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral That Represents the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179482 :question/id #uuid "c427d9b4-2944-5edc-8e19-363de348d23d" :question/math-academy-id "e-15147" :question/problem "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative\n\nII. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nIII. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\n![](images/7e/7e7fbe6aaf1310b186344a3cf5a127075f11a86b51db2e687ddc7035d5d890f8.png)" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C\\mathbin:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero, the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nThe outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below we see that $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to $\\mathbf n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a3d9864bb6bada1fd218b299427bc5bb4ffcbcccb1a504fff77416d5d292151.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/93/9310d17389f61de2984c0e23cfe1060e77d4a87a140d2f1c892bdbab9b99629b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049212 :topic/math-academy-id 3145 :topic/title "Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049653 :topic/math-academy-id 1285 :topic/title "Calculating a Scalar Projection"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050104 :topic/math-academy-id 1401 :topic/title "Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vectors in 2D"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f54ef5-437d-5af8-a751-b33cd1dcdd2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459517} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca4da76-b626-5282-9e3e-c1842c95aac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ca4da76-b626-5282-9e3e-c1842c95aac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459518} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ea5b52-19ab-545d-b803-91faf4690e76" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460625} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a9ad46d-5411-5071-a050-a7cba8cd0ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459519} {:answer/id #uuid "e615d0a2-c730-5dba-9263-c8a081802791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a9ad46d-5411-5071-a050-a7cba8cd0ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459519} :answer-field/id #uuid "743e0df3-174a-5257-a160-51980afa07d4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460626} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021ca85e-dc58-5e81-8883-f8dafe828184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459521} {:answer/id #uuid "2081d764-18e3-59ca-9a1b-b5feb261819b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2081d764-18e3-59ca-9a1b-b5feb261819b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459522} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b26c2a4-4801-5c19-84fb-9b2aa432ffa6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460627}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2d693ae-0e11-561b-9b69-4ee74bfcc078" :question/math-academy-id "q-126726" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e679efaa2612d45bbfe8b918de7148853b64eaf530af34a525ef1735e0946b09.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/fb/fbcd6fd0fadcc955212a145de9edb4654ddb92ddebb8ba31eb775cff676efeeb.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/a3/a3d398d382e6bc94ddde77e650d50a6b5ec050083f89f64104f6f2fd172a9239.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e48c47-8ee2-519f-87f2-b570d3b8dba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459523} {:answer/id #uuid "cc34336b-2e10-5376-83aa-f53156f87b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10e48c47-8ee2-519f-87f2-b570d3b8dba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459523} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e423910-3dd4-5ab2-a129-1141a47b6bb0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460628} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bc0bec0-62de-5062-ba34-89678d7a79ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459525} {:answer/id #uuid "970b48b7-8b9c-59fe-a14e-26cd96ab46a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459526}] :answ