b0cfa499c70547046318a29da6d9af7f556a07bcac830ed.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af413c3-686c-5c2d-bd12-502defdc9a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463604} {:answer/id #uuid "278d4075-be3f-56a0-8ec4-3e7094bec024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4af413c3-686c-5c2d-bd12-502defdc9a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463604} :answer-field/id #uuid "2137d776-bbde-5eeb-966a-9a2b6a799b38" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464736} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9512c2ad-6323-5b3d-99cc-416c3e5fc2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463606} {:answer/id #uuid "82f0e2e4-094c-54e4-8fe5-dcd89dd04ef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9512c2ad-6323-5b3d-99cc-416c3e5fc2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463606} :answer-field/id #uuid "51d19a22-2c6f-5c38-a191-01fc2a500890" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464737} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e64c3d63-ef55-5f3e-bd1f-c776629cc749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463608} {:answer/id #uuid "9f89e298-102b-5364-846f-ceb612b0dd6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f89e298-102b-5364-846f-ceb612b0dd6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463609} :answer-field/id #uuid "441f3c55-cced-5818-b31e-226165dab266" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464738}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e84dd576-6f5a-58c5-bf96-4c1a434830ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-127029" :question/problem "![](images/06/06c6dd24948a95d08850455d273c3456076850a47c980a086f06813b96bfd6ed.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement III is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/29/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/d7/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5323d7-d924-5036-a404-e42edaf50898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463610} {:answer/id #uuid "8215cbff-cc90-5f37-8a5a-22a2f081bfef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8215cbff-cc90-5f37-8a5a-22a2f081bfef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463611} :answer-field/id #uuid "aff70dd5-877c-59ef-81bc-8178f3f49dec" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464739} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d6f8d00-d75f-502f-bc54-6f1ccf5e06f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463612} {:answer/id #uuid "aac8e4b0-3d27-5f75-bde9-f37d964b4094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aac8e4b0-3d27-5f75-bde9-f37d964b4094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463613} :answer-field/id #uuid "82e64186-1575-540c-b2e9-ea65c962ca59" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464740} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46fa00f6-11fc-5e06-be3e-f6dba2027667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463614} {:answer/id #uuid "10df1e34-71fc-5eaf-b9c8-3da308db6b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46fa00f6-11fc-5e06-be3e-f6dba2027667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463614} :answer-field/id #uuid "8632d559-2742-5101-9099-c7c7ae2c0458" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464741}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92477238-fcf4-57c0-a788-d4cbd2b1a3bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-127030" :question/problem "![](images/65/6504ab3dde96670102e5046887707416495895dc3b85a514b5bbe7b526b4fa42.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/9a/9af12f28947e46810074574dad9f6b9a8b572f116456807bf9c478d12da19ec2.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/c4/c4402595afc4ce12937794f0755a446ae738d3b47d7e9f4978b6344f9ef8909a.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596e2f25-6344-5eef-8a75-204c85175502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463616} {:answer/id #uuid "f2be65cc-9a49-5f0b-8eef-59b759de229f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "596e2f25-6344-5eef-8a75-204c85175502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463616} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7d9b2de-be53-5786-80d4-7cae45fb50a3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464742} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "497c22f7-4edc-50c1-acb2-19f74b2baca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463618} {:answer/id #uuid "e235a714-1f0d-523a-8c12-6f930cfc72c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e235a714-1f0d-523a-8c12-6f930cfc72c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463619} :answer-field/id #uuid "45cc04e2-3594-5873-8ddb-dade30e36c6f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464743} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce71fa89-a63c-5932-a85a-15e6f162c9e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463620} {:answer/id #uuid "7ffa6d1d-e27b-5ef6-bf18-d9dcd01130db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ffa6d1d-e27b-5ef6-bf18-d9dcd01130db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463621} :answer-field/id #uuid "78427e4a-7c4c-55d1-bdbd-e9b61519ab8b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464744}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6542e3fc-27ed-5a91-abb0-bbb5f848f669" :question/math-academy-id "q-127032" :question/problem "![](images/45/453633f6309b9f1d2a3f7a8d4266ff10f571823cffd705d0bc93b930dce63d8f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/84/844d5e5574571ae221a86f541820a4b215766451031f4eea678cee46a1b83e41.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/b8/b8b31d46646db2d88d8a3f5658ee876383795ad5ba9aa55eb9959d5f95c02dbe.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1e6a479-52d1-5ae4-939a-e3b5d03fd778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474284} {:answer/id #uuid "4890dcc8-ff49-5248-a3fb-0fe1da9aeb4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1e6a479-52d1-5ae4-939a-e3b5d03fd778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474284} :answer-field/id #uuid "f527906f-2d30-55d6-998b-e4e8bf90fc09" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475068} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb84b4f7-0bc5-5c5a-9056-122bbc70bc57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474286} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9a6aa9-acc4-5992-a14e-804d5a9c01a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb84b4f7-0bc5-5c5a-9056-122bbc70bc57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474286} :answer-field/id #uuid "65cb578f-78b5-5225-966a-db5a469b668e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475069} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fac217d6-6ce8-5003-9d34-2c9ea1c6c89d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474288} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc8acfa-931e-5f66-bebf-19feca6c0ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcc8acfa-931e-5f66-bebf-19feca6c0ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474289} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d35692f-6bb0-56b8-9a68-82c51fb3c32c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475070}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ae03bac-e2a8-5c3b-a5da-8c8f23590a03" :question/math-academy-id "q-127026" :question/problem "![](images/28/28a7eb32cfcd800c907e9f4af927eca7bd8616d36162682413db62a9feabefbb.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/fe/fecf59674259d41222ffd981b1ab6d90c474fe2b0e37a6d5f7626f34536a6896.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/56/56a7fc09ed2a2a15b0949fa6041dc9c0ee6beb69f30b1a5c048afd8c57887d52.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2126782-b8eb-539b-8a44-4b656f8cdc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474278} {:answer/id #uuid "bc560dd6-7ac6-5a76-9df4-70c53f25e56e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2126782-b8eb-539b-8a44-4b656f8cdc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474278} :answer-field/id #uuid "94621230-a9f0-50e7-b575-e0567a8c5e06" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475065} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43e5a016-6e60-5487-bdda-a02e34603edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474280} {:answer/id #uuid "52b18bb0-cfb3-5c9f-948f-ea3749f36b0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52b18bb0-cfb3-5c9f-948f-ea3749f36b0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474281} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d370b0-1e92-5c62-966f-578bba64458f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475066} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137d1b3d-a1ad-5c7d-a87f-d48c60a7cbf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474282} {:answer/id #uuid "6c50f32b-3afd-545c-a6cd-342f755fc462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "137d1b3d-a1ad-5c7d-a87f-d48c60a7cbf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474282} :answer-field/id #uuid "3241e849-1133-58ff-a9e9-26f068ba66c0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475067}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cc7cc51-6281-53f8-b74f-55c2a0b2fa6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127024" :question/problem "![](images/01/01d8d85064253b9241b1fd93c22381897f23556d6e02c5c2c0161c94cee3bbdf.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *in* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb4fd4068760bb9132052b26508f0e1e0c02c45d425770de804b17ce7c512919.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/46/46afd4cde01c95388c9daefbef0668ef696d5817ab9ea3614982edf58a9bdf55.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86148950-3fc2-5f38-81ce-53ec93e76166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474290} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c0a1d4-d321-51db-9f9a-4313b742971e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86148950-3fc2-5f38-81ce-53ec93e76166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474290} :answer-field/id #uuid "eff65640-e27c-5010-a702-518eac2480f9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa09f0c0-3016-5c6e-b7c4-55a245d9d0ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474292} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c0e836-a991-5f1a-91a3-7effd835eb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0c0e836-a991-5f1a-91a3-7effd835eb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474293} :answer-field/id #uuid "176d727d-d760-55bb-bfd3-626eda4dc691" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475072} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7817b13e-8e9a-530f-860c-d01aa3b29a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474294} {:answer/id #uuid "419f49db-2832-5c8c-8fb3-49f7638b7dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "419f49db-2832-5c8c-8fb3-49f7638b7dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474295} :answer-field/id #uuid "912976bb-a0fa-5d56-b533-8354e5ec824a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475073}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e18756f-1e50-5f09-be71-26f001c4f68e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127027" :question/problem "![](images/af/afaec113101f1a63134d2b1cb5c9744b93f9b84dabafaa205780d2dc5b49a7bc.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/6b/6b24ece279cc480f20d7a4dbc65f7a5257af71f22688b1416c73e3b40673a321.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/9e/9e8527d26f0f2bd5372d8643f8f9321ec022bb0079e1fc5b23be4978f436613c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fc352a7-def2-5836-9338-8008f169268f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474296} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7131d4-fc3c-5f44-9a24-a7c8c38852ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d7131d4-fc3c-5f44-9a24-a7c8c38852ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474297} :answer-field/id #uuid "72b3e34c-4c68-5a6b-8479-025af4a4eb97" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475074} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "367f3fa0-3258-53ba-b118-21195c91f07c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474298} {:answer/id #uuid "8135c933-85bc-5657-aea9-2ece1f6a47c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "367f3fa0-3258-53ba-b118-21195c91f07c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474298} :answer-field/id #uuid "91409016-0963-5d73-b7be-10a1f16408c7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475075} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd98a563-23aa-5acb-89d3-93294ee5cc96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474300} {:answer/id #uuid "60ef0350-a526-513b-87b0-99da9194b0ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd98a563-23aa-5acb-89d3-93294ee5cc96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474300} :answer-field/id #uuid "17b07bc2-ae25-563c-8ed2-caa1721146f6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475076}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "765f030b-c4cb-527c-8c26-c947b0ace6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127028" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f1d25942ae680c068b587f38bfbc968c8850b8fa2db2003e994e1ec32dad892.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7acc3028de66ae71ae4e425da639e51a357a77f2b0a345290592cac91ba17d3.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/04/04639a0074aa4607fa57bfe589aad1780f8da6f573d8589a68c2fdb569b5a129.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179646 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179484 :question/id #uuid "f4a82102-6dd6-5a73-8370-9050e0f2323a" :question/math-academy-id "e-15149" :question/problem "![](images/05/05c91d681a0c84bbe9d8b6cab9bd8cdf1dca8bf88c851b8c7a7045f73ff9844c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides $C_2, C_3,$ and $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 4\\cdot 4 = 16.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048512 :topic/math-academy-id 3699 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Line Segments"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320053d8-7061-5bee-a125-4b5572fa81bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260325} {:answer/id #uuid "7754550d-7915-5725-8462-298bbf3140d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260917} {:answer/id #uuid "89563c80-e409-593a-b874-aab987bd64d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261072} {:answer/id #uuid "906a6921-6dc7-5a65-9058-0fd796a13a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261132} {:answer/id #uuid "cd431924-213d-5018-895a-46685548488d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "906a6921-6dc7-5a65-9058-0fd796a13a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261132} :answer-field/id #uuid "001f66bd-2162-519e-af79-734f33a25866" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262121}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea876586-e09c-5050-847d-bc8b312ea302" :question/math-academy-id "q-126881" :question/problem "![](images/19/19fca0188077e56d6b56048d1cd48ea840922cafe7873aaa02937c8c2845e998.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=2\\,j$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc34a4ce-679b-5695-9dfe-540cd60117ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177922} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fed570-9a0a-5fb6-b092-18090d3757e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177923} {:answer/id #uuid "034cd8e5-64a2-50b6-bec2-58e1146cb7e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177924} {:answer/id #uuid "64ad22d1-d74c-5598-8e39-3026099a604c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177925} {:answer/id #uuid "73d700d6-ddb9-5085-a028-fa38b1441baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177926}] :answer-field/id #uuid "268bf099-9758-5e4a-bc30-751c0618a551" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177921}] :question/id #uuid "30107d9c-6bc8-5ab5-a34a-0024b1e426f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127199" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c8aef7238da3bb5aed0fd2a458f6fd9bcd42884bbd350616e58e7a25b1f0fe2.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F =-2xi - 2yj$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262661 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18620d11-ce60-57c2-a580-d44a5579200c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260090} {:answer/id #uuid "3218e783-1e46-5bd3-bd29-5548bb105ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260326} {:answer/id #uuid "76437869-a2cd-55ab-8ebf-6ce70a057af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260908} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4d8c81-c3e3-5854-a2b5-53f7aae6f342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261523} {:answer/id #uuid "e35d583f-1e94-5ce0-ace0-3d795dc7095a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18620d11-ce60-57c2-a580-d44a5579200c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260090} :answer-field/id #uuid "95cb0a17-877b-5728-bbcf-86808ed0219e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262393}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ae07821-3e5f-5e7b-b438-d7c5726b59a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126890" :question/problem "![](images/70/70a51bcda43cd04a56c6b87a10723258c995e8daae87689a2ae8af5f44112ead.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 8=32.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b4648b-d247-5f92-9d2b-f244cac28ba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259878} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1a741e-d70d-5584-8f47-17f6fe12b753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260521} {:answer/id #uuid "59ab7c77-806c-599b-bff5-2271fa35fc54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260640} {:answer/id #uuid "825a056c-6bef-533e-873b-b5c171a4706c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261008} {:answer/id #uuid "ed84226c-f819-56cf-82e3-e3cbf03ace66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b1a741e-d70d-5584-8f47-17f6fe12b753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260521} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d3d48da-ade1-5236-99b3-6c5eeeefbc63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262283}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8d84a8f-b961-5115-a51c-b62b1cb780d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127190" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea53225d28411f38006861d6c8dc46f8d3d1ea01b3bb6183954f68e7124c5682.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=3\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009f40ac-91df-5ff9-993b-186929aec51b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259876} {:answer/id #uuid "8d61b731-ab18-54f1-93e0-e5ca68013f8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261112} {:answer/id #uuid "9060bbb8-05be-51ce-a85b-98fdecf11014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261131} {:answer/id #uuid "e48093a6-59c4-5f9e-8abb-72c28b727a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261884} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb8b743-8dc5-5437-83b1-ac7f99241c0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9060bbb8-05be-51ce-a85b-98fdecf11014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261131} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f0f2493-5bc7-580a-b18f-50e22975871f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262206}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "622ab5d8-9ae6-5da5-96af-e733a10932b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-127194" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8e1767b9bea45e742df4953f0ec50b2d43c9622994035342307dc2be6abba8de.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-2\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/87/878a9992405e5968a03a04d12f42aeb85bca52c07dc7081244b04011643a74bf.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=-2\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net outflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d550e73-f433-5554-9114-a2261ec35129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260128} {:answer/id #uuid "6db551f8-65db-52e2-b235-316483262543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/va