a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-8)=-32.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27376309-d644-597d-a2c9-90859cc06cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288291} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfba689-75b2-5e71-83fa-300f0b52711b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288343} {:answer/id #uuid "66be0097-da64-5230-a199-d4415883412f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288751} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ac569a-5062-5f6f-ab92-813702cf3b7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289151} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a1e55a-e608-50ab-be11-0613ec305f42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5a1e55a-e608-50ab-be11-0613ec305f42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289232} :answer-field/id #uuid "39f8f410-9e95-5f99-a90d-85e470264e2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289855}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10d8ca93-3d42-5558-a25c-f6c42ee07223" :question/math-academy-id "q-226126" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f827dac7ecaf3a659245d1178aa39a519ae3a40339c917004247f12554c871a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2x\\,i+2y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b753318ebad9270c3fc8788d0e3949ffb29a800eb3f8dd6fb67cb271be68033.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-4,4].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-4}^{4}4\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8(4-(-4)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 64=256.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47a0ff6d-e708-565d-a280-3cadd01ffb31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420867} {:answer/id #uuid "7d25776a-11ff-529c-8f88-125b184a30fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420878} {:answer/id #uuid "d86896fc-5499-5228-a1a9-ce525916fc2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420893} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc47f6e-dbd1-5cc9-912f-6e7a2036089f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420895} {:answer/id #uuid "f9651ba3-7ceb-59ea-8fd1-b5bacff9edfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d86896fc-5499-5228-a1a9-ce525916fc2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420893} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd37f018-666a-5dbb-8a05-7813cb84c875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420908}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f447fce-ea84-5549-9b80-d5d43d163662" :question/math-academy-id "q-226132" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f413fd04759cdb02118b320fea2f678712681a57f5b08e1bcb57a6843c0c80f0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=4x\\,i+4y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/87/878a9992405e5968a03a04d12f42aeb85bca52c07dc7081244b04011643a74bf.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(4x\\,i+4y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =4{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12(3-(-3)) \\\\  & =12\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 72=288.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "754059dd-7704-537c-8f5b-83a69628877d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420875} {:answer/id #uuid "76a9acd9-336e-553e-ac03-9d29b38dee70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420876} {:answer/id #uuid "b1bbaa21-1588-571b-867f-6c07807e4cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420885} {:answer/id #uuid "c4eec9b3-71ac-57f5-835f-95347213cc06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420888} {:answer/id #uuid "d5afc158-099d-5fca-ba5b-ba5702d29c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4eec9b3-71ac-57f5-835f-95347213cc06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420888} :answer-field/id #uuid "e89aabf0-d356-583a-a3cf-404273cddd70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420907}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4ef9955-c544-5a0b-9748-af661e7750b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-226115" :question/problem "![](images/57/575463a6fbe18d3f9b805e1a9c3d8e52bedd84a8db3a3c90d87381b8cb7abf74.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-4x\\,i-4y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/eb/ebe05a522f9f46acfed63e29927b46f41d78483b5df5e7a15a15ae5f33abd2f4.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-4x\\,i-4y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-8)=-32.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Square Using Symmetry"}]}
